<h2>The applications and the objection</h2>
<p>The Union&rsquo;s miscellaneous applications (M.A. Nos. 3313&ndash;3315 and 3316&ndash;3318 of 2026) were filed in civil appeals the Court had dismissed on 11 March 2026. The Solicitor General pressed for prospective operation and for time to conduct an equivalence-of-posts exercise. The respondents&rsquo; objection was structural: the applications were a disguised rehearing that circumvented the review jurisdiction, were filed 166 days late, and came from a litigant that had not performed the very exercise it had earlier assured the Court it would undertake.</p>

<h2>The Court&rsquo;s framework</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Entertain narrowly.</strong> The applications were taken up only &ldquo;for the said limited purpose&rdquo; of clarifying the operative directions; they &ldquo;do not warrant adjudication of any fresh cause of action&rdquo; (para 11).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Correct the premise.</strong> The Union&rsquo;s alarm rested on a misreading: &ldquo;This Court did not direct the retrospective revision or reconsideration of selections&rdquo; (para 15).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>State the rule.</strong> &ldquo;Miscellaneous applications cannot be employed as a means to seek a rehearing of matters already decided&rdquo; (para 16) — their province is clerical or arithmetical errors and genuine implementation difficulties.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Dispose with precision.</strong> Concluded selections stand: the ratio &ldquo;shall not, by itself, furnish a ground for reopening selections or appointments&rdquo; completed before 11 March 2026 (para 18(v)); pending allocations follow the judgment; compliance time extended by two months (para 19); the two-year window refused.</div>
</div>

<h2>The post-judgment toolkit, compared</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Remedy</th><th>What it is for</th><th>What it cannot do</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Miscellaneous application</td><td>Clerical/arithmetical corrections; clarification of operative directions; genuine implementation difficulties</td><td>Reargue the merits; introduce a fresh cause of action; obtain substantive modification</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Review (Article 137)</td><td>Error apparent on the face of the record; discovery of new and important matter</td><td>Function as an appeal in disguise</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Curative petition</td><td>Violation of natural justice; undisclosed judicial interest — the rarest corrective</td><td>Serve as a third round on the merits</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The Bench&rsquo;s approach also carries a message for government litigants in particular: an assurance recorded in the main judgment — here, the equivalence exercise — is expected to be performed within the time granted, and a miscellaneous application is not a device for purchasing further time on the strength of non-performance. The Court relied on its earlier statements of the principle, including <em>Jaipur Vidyut Vitran Nigam Ltd v. Adani Power Rajasthan Ltd</em> and <em>Ajay Kumar Jain v. State of U.P.</em>, alongside the foundational reservation jurisprudence of <em>Indra Sawhney</em>.</p>

<h2>Practical significance</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Parties facing adverse Supreme Court judgments should choose the correct corrective remedy early: the limitation clock for review runs from the judgment, and an MA filed in its place buys no extension.</p>
  <p>Where a judgment genuinely creates implementation problems — timelines that cannot be met, directions whose scope is disputed in execution — an MA remains the right vehicle, and this decision shows the Court will engage with it to the extent of clarification and modest extension.</p>
  <p>For those defending a favourable judgment, the decision supplies ready language: an application seeking to unsettle the ratio or reopen settled consequences is objectionable at the threshold, without reaching its merits.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a reported judgment of the Supreme Court for general information. The underlying service-law dispute has its own complexities, and affected candidates or departments should assess their position on the full text.</div>
